me{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation II has no solutions." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186403919 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4]?$\n\nI. $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$\nII. $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$\nIII. $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186403919 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4]?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186403919 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$ is not defined at $x=0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-4,4].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ is continuous on $[-4,\\infty )$ and differentiable on $(-4,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ and $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4].$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186403919 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$ is not defined at $x=0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-4,4].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ is continuous on $[-4,\\infty )$ and differentiable on $(-4,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4].$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186404044 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-xy$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186404044 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186404058 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{4t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(t)={e}^{-t-1}$\nII. $y(t)=7{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-4t}$\nIII. $y(t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{4}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [1759