ime $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186404672 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nTherefore, only function $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$ could be used to represent  $y(t).$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186404672 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t$\n\nTherefore, only function I could be used to represent  $y(t).$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186404679 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+{ϕ}_{1})$\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186404679 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186404679 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nTherefore, only statement $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$ could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186404679 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{5}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{5}t$\n\nTherefore, only statement II could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186405016 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186405016 1071 "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186405016 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186405016 1074 "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$”,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$”, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence “${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 2$” diverges." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186405146 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices have coefficient matrices that are **not** in row echelon form? Treat the final column of each augmented matrix as its constants column.\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccccc|c}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186405146 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices have coefficient matrices that are **not** in row echelon form? Treat the final column of each augmented matrix as its constants column.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186406655 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186406655 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n- **U.** $U={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **W.** $W={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **V.** $V={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186406841 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186406841 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- **II.** If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **III.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186406841 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186406841 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$” is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible” is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186406843 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ 