$k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $2∣(60x-45y)$ is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement $9∣(60x-45y)$ is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement $5∣(60x-45y)$ is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" $5∣(60x-45y)$ only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186407498 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186407499 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186407499 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5∣(8x+6y)$\n- **II.** $2∣(8x+6y)$\n- **III.** $3∣(8x+6y)$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186407499 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n 