c{7}{{x}^{8}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186408038 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186408038 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” and ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$”; ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409432 1071 "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409432 1071 "Given that $26≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n- **I.** $n=9$\n- **II.** $n=8$\n- **III.** $n=3$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409432 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409432 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=26-8=18.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $9∣18$ because $18÷9=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $8∤18.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $3∣18$ because $18÷3=6,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=9$; $n=3$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409433 1071 "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409433 1071 "Given that $29≡-16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n- **I.** $n=5$\n- **II.** $n=8$\n- **III.** $n=9$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409433 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409433 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=29-(-16)=45.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∣45$ because $45÷5=9,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $8∤45.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=8.$\n\n- Note that $9∣45$ because $45÷9=5,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=5$; $n=9$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409434 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409434 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- **II.** $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- **III.** $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409434 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case $11≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$ gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case $18≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $24≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409434 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a false statement because $3∤(11-3).$ Notice that $11-3=8,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $4∤(18-16).$ Notice that $18-16=2,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $5∣(24-19).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(24-19)÷5=5÷5=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186409435 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186409435 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\