c vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement $Pq=q$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.03+0.72 \\\\ 0.07+0.18\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$” is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$q$ is a stochastic vector” only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411677 1071 "Given the relation $R$ on $N$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x>y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411677 1071 "Given the relation $R$ on $N$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x>y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $6\\,R\\,8$\n\n- **II.** $7\\,R\\,2$\n\n- **III.** $9\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,1$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411677 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x>y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $>$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $≯$ (or, equivalently, $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\le $) are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement $6\\,R\\,8$ is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $6>8,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement $7\\,R\\,2$ is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $>,$ we have $7>2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement $9\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,1$ is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\le ,$ we have $9\\le 1,$ which