I.** $1\\,R\\,9$\n\n- **III.** $4\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,13$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411679 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ or equivalently, $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $6∣(x-y-4).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement $0\\,R\\,2$ is true. In this case, we have $6∣(0-2-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement $1\\,R\\,9$ is true. In this case, we have $6∣(1-9-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement $4\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,13$ is true. In this case, we have $6∤(4-13-4),$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $0\\,R\\,2$; $1\\,R\\,9$; $4\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,13$." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411679 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ or equivalently, $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $6∣(x-y-4).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. In this case, we have $6∣(0-2-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. In this case, we have $6∣(1-9-4),$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, we have $6∤(4-13-4),$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411856 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411856 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ must have a complex eigenvalue\n\n- **II.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n\n- **III.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411856 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411856 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have a complex eigenvalue” is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not diagonalizable” is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411865 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186411865 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411865 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$ is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthogonal” is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthonormal” is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186411865 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412004 1071 "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412004 1071 "Given that $7≡19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n- **I.** $n=5$\n- **II.** $n=6$\n- **III.** $n=7$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412004 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412004 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=7-19=-12.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $5∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=5.$\n\n- Note that $6∣(-12)$ because $(-12)÷6=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=6.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-12).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=6$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412006 1071 "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412006 1071 "Given that $15≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n- **I.** $n=2$\n- **II.** $n=4$\n- **III.** $n=6$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412006 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412006 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=15-7=8.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣8$ because $8÷2=4,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $4∣8$ because $8÷4=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=4.$\n\n- Note that $6∤8.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=2$; $n=4$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412007 1071 "Given that $-7≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412007 1071 "Given that $-7≡-3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n- **I.** $n=2$\n- **II.** $n=3$\n- **III.** $n=7$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412007 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-7-(-3)=-4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-4)$ because $(-4)÷2=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412007 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-7-(-3)=-4.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∣(-4)$ because $(-4)÷2=-2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $7∤(-4).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $n=2$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412061 1071 "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412061 1071 "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$\n\n- **II.** $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412061 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$ is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$; $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412061 1074 "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412421 1071 "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412421 1071 "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- **II.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has no solution" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412421 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412421 1074 "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement “The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point” is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist” is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has no solution” is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point” is true." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412805 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412805 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$\n\n- **II.** $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n\n- **III.** $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412805 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\\times {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Option $x(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412805 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-3,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412961 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412961 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** There are $3$ distinct residue classes modulo $6$\n\n- **II.** $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}\\ne ∅$\n\n- **III.** $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412961 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412961 1074 "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “There are $3$ distinct residue classes modulo $6$” is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}\\ne ∅$ is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$ is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Z=[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2}$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412964 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[4]_{7}?$\n\nI. $-3$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $18$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412964 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[4]_{7}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412964 1074 "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412964 1074 "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number $-3$”; “The number $18$” only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412965 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $6$ contain the integer $7?$\n\nI. $[-5]_{6}$\n\nII. $[3]_{6}$\n\nIII. $[1]_{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186412965 1071 "Which of the following residue classes modulo $6$ contain the integer $7?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186412965 1074 "We have $7\\in [a]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\n7≡a\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence c