 function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413343 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413394 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413394 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\n- **III.** $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413394 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413394 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$” is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$”; “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”; “$f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413395 1071 "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413395 1071 "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\n- **II.** $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\n- **III.** $g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413395 1074 "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413395 1074 "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement “$g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413605 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413605 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **II.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **III.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413605 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413605 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413606 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413606 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413606 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ and ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}^{′}(t