ered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(4,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $4$-row and the $3$-column.\n\n- The pair $(8,6)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $8$-row and the $6$-column.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $2$-row and the $3$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(8,6)$; $(2,3)$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413744 1071 "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413744 1071 "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- **II.** The planes intersect on a line.\n\n- **III.** The planes are coincident." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413744 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413744 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$” is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement “The planes are coincident” is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413824 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413824 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186413825 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}-5 & 0 & 8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}4 & 0 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & -9 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186413825 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414080 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $2x+5y=200?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414080 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $2x+5y=200?$\n- **I.** $(20,32)$\n- **II.** $(10,20)$\n- **III.** $(15,35)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414080 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(20,32)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=20$ and $y=32,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 20+5\\cdot 32 & =200 \\\\ 40+160 & =200 \\\\ 200 & =200\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(10,20)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=10$ and $y=20,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 10+5\\cdot 20 & =200 \\\\ 20+100 & =200 \\\\ 120 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(15,35)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=15$ and $y=35,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 15+5\\cdot 35 & =200 \\\\ 30+175 & =200 \\\\ 205 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414080 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(20,32)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=20$ and $y=32,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 20+5\\cdot 32 & =200 \\\\ 40+160 & =200 \\\\ 200 & =200\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(10,20)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=10$ and $y=20,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 10+5\\cdot 20 & =200 \\\\ 20+100 & =200 \\\\ 120 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(15,35)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=15$ and $y=35,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =200 \\\\ 2\\cdot 15+5\\cdot 35 & =200 \\\\ 30+175 & =200 \\\\ 205 & \\ne 200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(20,32)$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414082 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $5x+6y=8?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414082 1071 "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $5x+6y=8?$\n- **I.** $(-2,2)$\n- **II.** $(-8,8)$\n- **III.** $(4,-4)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414082 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 2 & =8 \\\\ -10+12 & =8 \\\\ 2 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(-8,8)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=-8$ and $y=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-8)+6\\cdot 8 & =8 \\\\ -40+48 & =8 \\\\ 8 & =8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-4) & =8 \\\\ 20-24 & =8 \\\\ -4 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414082 1074 "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 2 & =8 \\\\ -10+12 & =8 \\\\ 2 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(-8,8)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=-8$ and $y=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-8)+6\\cdot 8 & =8 \\\\ -40+48 & =8 \\\\ 8 & =8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(4,-4)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=4$ and $y=-4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+6y & =8 \\\\ 5\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-4) & =8 \\\\ 20-24 & =8 \\\\ -4 & \\ne 8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(-8,8)$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414084 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414084 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n- **I.** $15x-20y=5$\n- **II.** $11x+9y=13$\n- **III.** $-8x+2y=5$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414084 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation $15x-20y=5$ has solutions. We have $a=15,$ $b=-20,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(15,-20)=5\n$$\n\nand $5\\,|\\,5,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation $11x+9y=13$ has solutions. We have $a=11,$ $b=9,$ and $c=13.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,9)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,13,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation $-8x+2y=5$ has no solution. We have $a=-8,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-8,2)=2\n$$\n\nand $2\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,5,$ our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $15x-20y=5$; $11x+9y=13$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414084 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=15,$ $b=-20,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(15,-20)=5\n$$\n\nand $5\\,|\\,5,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has solutions. We have $a=11,$ $b=9,$ and $c=13.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,9)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,13,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=-8,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-8,2)=2\n$$\n\nand $2\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,5,$ our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414086 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414086 1071 "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n- **I.** $3x+7y=11$\n- **II.** $-3x-9y=2$\n- **III.** $-12x+15y=21$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414086 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation $3x+7y=11$ has solutions. We have $a=3,$ $b=7,$ and $c=11.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,11,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation $-3x-9y=2$ has no solution. We have $a=-3,$ $b=-9,$ and $c=2.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-3,-9)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,2,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation $-12x+15y=21$ has solutions. We have $a=-12,$ $b=15,$ and $c=21.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-12,15)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,|\\,21,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3x+7y=11$; $-12x+15y=21$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414086 1074 "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=3,$ $b=7,$ and $c=11.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,11,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=-3,$ $b=-9,$ and $c=2.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-3,-9)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,2,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-12,$ $b=15,$ and $c=21.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-12,15)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,|\\,21,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414417 1071 "$$\nX=\\{a,b,2,4\\},\\,Y=\\{n\\in N∣\\,1\\le n<5\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $X$ and $Y$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414417 1071 "$$\nX=\\{a,b,2,4\\},\\,Y=\\{n\\in N∣\\,1\\le n<5\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $X$ and $Y$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $|X|=4$\n\n- **II.** $|Y|=5$\n\n- **III.** $|X|=|Y|$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414417 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $|X|=4$ is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement $|Y|=5$ is false. Notice that $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement $|X|=|Y|$ is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $|X|=4$; $|X|=|Y|$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414417 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414421 1071 "$$\nS=\\{1,1,2,3,\\{4,5\\}\\},\\,T=\\{n\\in N∣\\,2\\le n\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $S$ and $T$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414421 1071 "$$\nS=\\{1,1,2,3,\\{4,5\\}\\},\\,T=\\{n\\in N∣\\,2\\le n\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $S$ and $T$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $|S|=4$\n\n- **II.** $|T|=5$\n\n- **III.** $|S|=|T|$" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414421 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement $|S|=4$ is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement $|T|=5$ is false. Notice that $T=\\{2,3,4,5,6,7\\}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement $|S|=|T|$ is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $|S|=4$ only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414421 1074 "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $T=\\{2,3,4,5,6,7\\}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414783 1071 "![](images/5a/5a3961346a4bcb0d94e46caf6d6ed1253dc56fb4761db07b11829e329bc2fa8d.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414783 1071 "![](images/5a/5a3961346a4bcb0d94e46caf6d6ed1253dc56fb4761db07b11829e329bc2fa8d.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414783 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](images/af/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a2/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only." 13194139537076 17592186576411 true] [17592186414783 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](images/af/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a2/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414784 1071 "![](images/8f/8ffc97d4fc442503eb5b59e01f317cc7a77cf57d041e16807aa0645f962a5aeb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the three-quarter circle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537076 17592186576411 false] [17592186414784 1071 "![](images/8f/8ffc97d4fc442503eb5b59e01f317cc7a77cf57d041e16807aa0645f962a5aeb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the three-quarter circle is traversed from $A$ to $B.